1
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,500
Hi, and welcome to the first episode of our journey into the mathematics of waves.

2
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:18,200
Waves are everywhere: sound, light, ocean swells, even the electrons in your phone behave like waves.

3
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:24,900
Before we can write down the wave equation, we need a small toolbox. Here's today's plan.

4
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,700
Let's go!

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,800
So, what is a wave? A good definition: a wave is a disturbance that travels, carrying energy from one place to another.

6
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,100
Look at this row of particles.

7
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:54,100
The pattern moves to the right... but keep your eye on the orange particle. It just bobs up and down!

8
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:00,000
The medium stays where it is. Only the disturbance, and its energy, travels along.

9
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:07,083
This is a transverse wave: the particles move perpendicular to the direction in which the wave travels.

10
00:01:07,383 --> 00:01:16,583
In a longitudinal wave, like sound in air, the particles jiggle back and forth along the direction of travel, making compressions and rarefactions.

11
00:01:17,866 --> 00:01:25,866
Two pictures help a lot. First, freeze time and take a snapshot of the wave: displacement y versus position x.

12
00:01:26,166 --> 00:01:33,766
The distance from one crest to the next is the wavelength, lambda. The largest displacement is the amplitude, A.

13
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:40,466
Now do the opposite: sit at one spot and record the displacement as time goes by.

14
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:50,366
The time for one full cycle is the period, T. Its inverse, one over T, is the frequency f: cycles per second, or hertz.

15
00:01:51,666 --> 00:01:57,666
Here's the key connection. In one period, the wave moves forward by exactly one wavelength.

16
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:03,966
So its speed is lambda over T, which is the same as lambda times f.

17
00:02:05,266 --> 00:02:14,466
Physicists love radians, so we also define the wavenumber k, two pi over lambda, and the angular frequency omega, two pi times f.

18
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:18,750
In this language the speed is simply omega over k.

19
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:31,850
Next up: vectors. A vector is a quantity with both a size and a direction, like the velocity of a wave, or the displacement of a particle.

20
00:02:32,150 --> 00:02:34,550
We draw it as an arrow.

21
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:42,450
Any vector in the plane can be built from two pieces, its components along the x and y axes.

22
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:53,150
With the unit vectors x hat and y hat, each of length one, we write: A equals A x, x hat, plus A y, y hat.

23
00:02:53,450 --> 00:03:00,250
Pythagoras gives its length, the magnitude. And a bit of trigonometry gives its direction, the angle theta.

24
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:05,150
Adding vectors is easy. Here's a second vector, B.

25
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:15,450
Slide B so that its tail sits on the tip of A. The sum goes from the start of A to the end of B.

26
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:20,550
In components you just add the x's together and the y's together.

27
00:03:21,833 --> 00:03:27,833
One more operation we'll need: the dot product. Multiply matching components, then add them up.

28
00:03:28,133 --> 00:03:37,333
Geometrically, it's the two lengths times the cosine of the angle between the vectors: it measures how much they point the same way.

29
00:03:37,616 --> 00:03:47,216
Why bother? Because in three dimensions the phase of a plane wave contains exactly such a dot product, k dot r. We'll get there!

30
00:03:49,516 --> 00:03:55,916
Now for something that sounds scary, but is actually a wave lover's best friend: complex numbers.

31
00:03:56,216 --> 00:04:07,016
It all starts with the imaginary unit i, defined so that i squared equals minus one. No real number can do that, so we simply invent one.

32
00:04:08,316 --> 00:04:15,516
A complex number z, a plus i b, has a real part, a, and an imaginary part, b.

33
00:04:15,816 --> 00:04:23,016
Use them as coordinates, and every complex number becomes a point, or an arrow, in the complex plane.

34
00:04:23,316 --> 00:04:33,316
Looks a lot like a 2D vector, right? The length of the arrow is the magnitude of z, and its angle is called the phase.

35
00:04:33,616 --> 00:04:41,616
So we can also write z using its length and its angle: magnitude, times cosine theta plus i sine theta.

36
00:04:43,916 --> 00:04:49,516
Here's the magic trick. Start from the number one, and multiply it by i.

37
00:04:54,616 --> 00:05:02,216
One, i, minus one, minus i, and back to one. Multiplying by i is a rotation by 90 degrees!

38
00:05:04,516 --> 00:05:10,516
In general, when you multiply two complex numbers, their magnitudes multiply and their angles add.

39
00:05:10,816 --> 00:05:18,416
Rotation and stretching, all in one operation. That's exactly why complex numbers are so handy for anything that oscillates.

40
00:05:20,716 --> 00:05:29,516
Last tool: the complex conjugate, z star. It flips the sign of the imaginary part: a mirror image across the real axis.

41
00:05:29,816 --> 00:05:39,016
And z times z star is the magnitude squared: always a real number. We'll use that a lot, for instance to compute intensities.

42
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:46,500
Now, perhaps the most beautiful formula you'll see in this course: Euler's relation.

43
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:57,200
Since cos squared plus sin squared is one, e to the i theta always sits on the unit circle, at angle theta from the real axis.

44
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:01,100
As theta grows, the point goes round and round.

45
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:10,200
Now watch its shadows. The horizontal shadow, the real part, is cosine theta. The vertical shadow, the imaginary part, is sine theta.

46
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:19,700
A rotating arrow secretly contains two oscillations, ninety degrees apart. Keep this picture in mind: it is the heart of the next episode.

47
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,800
Euler gives the neatest way to write any complex number: magnitude times e to the i theta.

48
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,100
And multiplying just adds the angles, simply because exponents add.

49
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:38,800
Euler also works backwards. Two half-length arrows spinning in opposite directions...

50
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:45,500
...always add up to a point on the real axis: their sum is exactly cosine theta.

51
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,800
A similar trick, with a minus sign, gives the sine.

52
00:06:51,100 --> 00:07:02,700
And a little bonus: set theta equal to pi. e to the i pi, plus one, equals zero. Five of the most important numbers in maths, in one line.

53
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:14,200
Finally, let's put things together and describe a wave with a function. The displacement y depends on position x and on time t.

54
00:07:14,483 --> 00:07:18,083
Take any shape f of x, like this pulse.

55
00:07:18,383 --> 00:07:28,783
Now replace x by x minus v t. As time goes on, the whole shape slides to the right at speed v, without changing its form.

56
00:07:29,083 --> 00:07:38,683
Why? Follow the peak. It sits where x minus v t keeps the same value, that is, at a point moving with speed v.

57
00:07:38,983 --> 00:07:45,383
Flip the sign, f of x plus v t, and the pulse travels to the left.

58
00:07:46,683 --> 00:07:57,883
The most important example of all is the harmonic wave: A, times the cosine of k x minus omega t, plus phi. A sine works just as well.

59
00:07:58,166 --> 00:08:09,366
It's f of x minus v t in disguise: k x minus omega t is k times x minus v t, with v equal to omega over k.

60
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:20,850
The whole argument of the cosine is called the phase. And phi, the phase constant, slides the wave along: it fixes where the wave starts at time zero.

61
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:34,133
And here's the payoff of Euler's relation: the same wave is the real part of A, e to the i, k x minus omega t.

62
00:08:34,433 --> 00:08:41,233
Behind every point of the wave, there's a little rotating arrow. That arrow is called a phasor.

63
00:08:41,516 --> 00:08:45,116
And phasors are exactly where we'll start next time.

64
00:08:47,416 --> 00:08:52,216
Let's recap. A wave is a travelling disturbance, with speed lambda f.

65
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,700
Vectors have components, a magnitude and a direction.

66
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Complex numbers live in a plane, and multiplying them rotates.

67
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:03,100
Euler connects exponentials with cosines and sines.

68
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:09,400
And any function of x minus v t is a wave travelling to the right.

69
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:15,300
Thanks for watching, and see you in episode two, where the phasors start spinning!

